In the deep fringe, something vast and patient is tucking a sleeping patient into the loam, petal by careful petal.
The Greenwatcher turns its great mild eyes on you. It is not afraid. It does not understand why you would be.
[It gestures, almost tenderly, at the half-planted Bloomwarden — see? warm. ready. loved.]
"..." — it has no words, only the Glut's first and oldest argument: everything wants to go back into the ground, and kindness is helping it arrive early.
[You do not draw a blade. You go low, and slow, and gentle — the way Wenlow asked. You offer it the mercy it keeps trying to give.]
It considers you for a long, green moment. Then it lifts the patient back out of the soil, and lays them in your arms instead.
[The Greenwatcher settles, pacified — not beaten. It will follow the kind ones now. It was only ever looking for someone to be gentle with it back.]
